<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kaykaus I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1220">1220</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Izz al-Din Kaykaus I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kaykaus I was sultan of the Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a> from 1211 to 1220. He expanded the sultanate to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, taking the port of Sinop in 1214 and capturing Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-of-trebizond">Alexios I of Trebizond</a>, whom he released as a tributary. He founded a hospital and medical school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a>, where he was buried.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaykaus-i.jpg" alt="Kaykaus I" width="320" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aramgar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sinop_bilingual_inscription_of_Kaykaus_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Kaykaus I was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaykhusraw-i">Kaykhusraw I</a>. When his father died at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alasehir">Alaşehir</a> in 1211, his two younger brothers, Kayferidun Ibrahim and the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayqubad-i">Kayqubad I</a>, challenged his succession. Kayqubad won some support from neighbours of the sultanate, among them Leo I, king of Cilician Armenia, and the independent ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Erzurum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughith-al-din-tughrilshah">Tughrilshah</a>, who was his uncle. Kayferidun put the newly acquired port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antalya">Antalya</a> at risk by seeking help from the Cypriot Franks.</p><p>Most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> supported Kaykaus, who seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayseri">Kayseri</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> from his base in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malatya">Malatya</a>, which brought Leo over to his side. He soon captured both brothers and secured the throne. He also made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-i-laskaris">Theodore Laskaris</a>, the Byzantine emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>, which ended hostilities between the two states.</p><p>His most significant gain was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> port of Sinop. In 1214 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> tribesmen captured Alexios, Grand Komnenos of the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a>, on a hunting trip outside the city and handed him to the sultan. He bought his freedom with Sinop and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> of the Trapezuntine territory to the east. The Seljuqs thus gained a Black Sea outlet to match <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antalya">Antalya</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and a wedge was driven between Trebizond and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>. Kaykaus appointed an Armenian, Rais Hetoum, to govern the mixed Greek and Turkish population, and ordered churches in the city converted to mosques. Between April and September 1215 the walls were rebuilt under the Greek architect Sebastos, with fifteen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> contributing to the cost.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kaykaus allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-afdal">al-Afdal</a>, an exiled Ayyubid and a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, who offered him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> in return for help in regaining his power. In 1216 Kaykaus attacked Aleppo in support of al-Afdal, his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, and was defeated by al-Ashraf. He was working towards an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badr-al-din-lulu">Badr al-Din Lu'lu'</a>, emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> when he died in 1220, and the alliance collapsed.</p><p>He built a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> in 1212 and in 1217 the Şifaiye Medresesi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a>, designed as a hospital and medical school. His mausoleum stands in its south eyvan under a conical dome, and the facade bears a poem by the sultan in blue faience tiles. According to Rustam Shukurov, Kaykaus and his brother Kayqubad, who spent considerable time in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> with their father, very probably shared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaykhusraw-i">Kaykhusraw I</a>'s dual Christian and Muslim and Turkic, Persian and Greek identity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kaykaus I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Theodore Laskaris took back Heraclea and the Paphlagonian lands, and in 1214 the Seljuk sultan <b>Kaykaus I</b> captured Sinop, taking Alexios prisoner and releasing him only as a tributary vassal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…1211 fighting the Empire of Nicaea at Antioch on the Meander, but his sons expanded to the coasts: <b>Kaykaus I</b> took Sinop and captured the emperor of Trebizond, and Kayqubad I fortified Alanya, building its Red Tower and a shipyard, sent an expedition across the Black Sea to…</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sultanate of Rum under <b>Kaykaus I</b> took Sinope, an important Black Sea port held by the Empire of Trebizond.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-1">1 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
